      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The first five prime numbers are 2, 3, 5, 7, and 11. The sum of these numbers is $2 + 3 + 5 + 7 + 11 = 28$. The sixth prime number is 13. To find the remainder when 28 is divided by 13, we can perform long division: \n\n\\[\\begin{array}{c}\n13)28 \\quad (2)\n\\\\\n\\\\\n13\n\\\\\n13\n\\\\\n\\hline\n5\n\\end{array}\\] \n\nThe remainder is 5. Therefore, the remainder when the sum of the first five primes is divided by the sixth prime is \\boxed{5}. Note: The problem did not require a numerical calculation, but rather an understanding of the problem statement and the remainder operation.\n\nThe sum problem was solved by the student using a traditional approach, but a more mathematical way to solve it is to use modular arithmetic:\n\nSince 28 and 13 are relatively prime (no common factor other than 1), by Fermat's Little Theorem, we know that:\n\n\\[28^{12} \\equiv 28 \\pmod{13}\\]\n\nHence,\n\n\\[28 \\cdot 28^{12} = 28^{13} \\equiv 28 \\cdot 28 \\equiv 28 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{13}\\]\n\nThe remainder is indeed 5, so the answer is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
